## 7. Limitations and caveats (mandatory section)

This section is **required**. The methodology's claim to
generalization is bounded by the artifacts that produced it; future
readers must encounter those bounds before they decide to trust the
prompt.

### 7.1 Model lock-in

This prompt was optimized against `{{MODEL_IDENTIFIER}}` exclusively.
Per DESIGN.md §2.2, model overfitting is contextually acceptable and
documented rather than prevented in v1. Specifically:

{{MODEL_LOCKIN_CAVEAT}}
<!-- MODEL_LOCKIN_CAVEAT: filled with the prompt's lock-in posture
     and known cross-model fragility, if any. If the user has not
     run the prompt against other models, state that explicitly:
     "this prompt has not been validated on any other model;
     swapping models requires re-running /spp-loop to verify."

     If cross-model evaluation HAS been performed (off the v1
     happy path but possible), name each model and the F1 delta,
     and surface failure-cluster persistence — see DESIGN.md §2.2's
     length-correlated cross-family example as the framing. Do not
     copy the source-project numbers as if they were this run's
     numbers (DESIGN.md §7.2). -->

### 7.2 Baseline scope and provenance

Baseline source: {{BASELINE_SOURCE_DESCRIPTION}}
Baseline size: {{BASELINE_SIZE}} rows ({{BASELINE_CLASS_BALANCE}})
Labeling provenance: {{BASELINE_LABELING_PROVENANCE}}
<!-- Filled from plan.md §6 + baseline-quality review notes. -->

The prompt's scores apply to data that resembles this baseline. If
the production data distribution drifts from the baseline (new
sources, new vocabulary, different time period), re-validation is
required.

### 7.3 Persistent failure clusters

The clusters in §4 are known failure modes that the loop did not
resolve. Production deployment must either (a) accept these failures
within the production decision rule's tolerance, or (b) install a
downstream guardrail. They are not bugs to fix in code review; they
are documented limitations.

### 7.4 Loop interruption posture (v1)

This prompt was produced under v1's loop posture: an interrupted
iteration is discarded and re-run. There is no mid-iteration
resumption (DESIGN.md §7.1 non-goals). Long loops may have been
re-run from the prior iteration's output if the user interrupted.
This does not affect score validity but is recorded for
traceability.

